WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.840
Powerful cosmic rays smash through
spaceships.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.830
But how can objects smaller than an atom?

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.460
Carry enough energy to be dangerous to
astronauts?

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.630
Moving objects carry energy.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.730
We call this kinetic energy.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:20.570
And when they strike something, they
transform that energy.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:29.900
When I hit my hand, the kinetic energy of my
fist transforms into sound and heat and

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.900
vibration. My hand hurts a little from that
impact from the transformation of energy.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.070
It's the same thing with cosmic rays.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.430
When they slam into a human brain cell or a
computer chip, they dump some energy causing

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:50.660
damage. How much damage depends on their
kinetic energy.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.410
And that comes down to two things mass and
speed.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.990
Intuitively, things that are moving at the
same speed.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.850
If they're more massive, they carry more
energy.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.550
A bigger asteroid slamming into Earth will
do more damage than a smaller asteroid.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.100
If you double the mass of an object, its
kinetic energy also doubles.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.020
Although mass is important, it's not as
important as speed.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.340
Speed matters even more than mass.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:29.280
The kinetic energy depends directly on the
mass, but it depends on the square of the

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.290
speed. Here's what that means.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.290
You double the mass, you have double the
kinetic energy.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.010
You double the speed you have four times the
kinetic energy.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.840
When it comes to speed.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.390
Cosmic rays are the elite.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:54.340
An ultra high energy cosmic ray detected in
1991 hit the atmosphere so fast, scientists

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.130
called it the Oh, my God particle.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.260
This particle was higher energy than they
thought they would ever, ever see.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.110
Until this fluorescent streak in the Utah
sky.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.570
No one believed the particle could reach the
earth, traveling so close to the speed of

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:18.820
light, making cosmic ray particles far more
dangerous than expected.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:24.730
As you approach the speed of light energy
momentum mass, they start to act a little bit

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:25.600
differently.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.090
Einstein's equations of relativity become
important.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.790
The physics changes and the energy it has
becomes much, much, much stronger.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.340
If a particle is moving at close to the speed
of light, that means that its energy is

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.670
almost at the maximum allowed by the laws of
physics.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.100
It's amazing to think that something as tiny
as a proton could actually be dangerous to

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:56.130
human being. But amazingly, that proton is
moving so fast it carries as much energy as a

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.410
baseball thrown at a hundred miles an hour.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.250
A baseball contains over 1,000,000,000,000
trillion protons.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.460
Imagine all that energy carried by just one
particle.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:09.090
So now you get a sense of just how risky
these can be.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:16.550
Ultra high energy cosmic rays like the oh my
God, particle are like supersonic missiles.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.750
They are the fastest, but they're so rare.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.400
Astronauts are unlikely to be hit by one.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.970
Solar cosmic rays are like baby pellets.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.150
Abundant, but our spacecraft can block them.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:34.070
The biggest threat to our astronauts,
however, are galactic cosmic rays.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.010
They come from elsewhere in the Milky Way.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.140
The combination of their speed and frequency
makes them the most dangerous.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:48.710
These galactic cosmic rays are much more
powerful than the solar cosmic rays, and

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:53.510
they've traveled enormous distances to mess
you up.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:01.420
Luckily, our astronauts have a surprising
protector, a guardian of the solar system,

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:02.860
the sun.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:10.130
As well as spitting out these high energy
solar cosmic ray particles.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:16.780
The sun is also streaming out lots of much
lower energy particles of the solar wind.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.020
That outward moving solar wind acts as a
force field in the cosmic rays have to work

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.450
their way upstream to get to Earth far
inside this bubble.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:35.350
The solar wind extends 11 billion miles
around the solar system, generating a

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:40.150
magnetic field that repels incoming galactic
cosmic rays.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.920
It's almost like the deflector shield of the
Starship Enterprise.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:52.810
So the sun's magnetic field partially helps
protect the Earth and any astronauts from the

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.010
incoming radiation.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:05:01.220
Not long ago, our Voyager spacecraft made it
to that boundary between the sun's bubble and

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.380
the galaxy and was able to study that
region.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.380
And we see the difference between inside the
sun's bubble and what's going on outside the

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:15.650
sun's bubble. The sun has our back billions
of miles away, and that's pretty cool.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.710
The Voyager space probes discovered a moving
battlefield.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.570
The solar wind behaves a bit like a storm
front on Earth.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.760
Sometimes it advances.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.090
Sometimes it retreats.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:36.050
When the sun's activity is the highest, it's
spitting out more solar energetic protons.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.130
But those solar cosmic rays are much less
damaging than the galactic ones.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.680
So the net is a benefit.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:49.550
So actually, ironically, you might find that
the astronauts, it is safer to launch

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:56.420
missions to Mars during a period of higher
solar activity because although you have more

00:05:56.450 --> 00:06:03.980
of the solar particle radiation, you also
get a better shielding effect from the solar

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:04.460
wind.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.830
The sun's activity goes through an 11 year
cycle of highs and lows.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:15.830
The protective bubble follows the same
cycle, allowing NASA to predict the safest

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:17.240
times to launch.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:23.030
This is a thorny problem and we have very
smart people working on it, but we want to

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:28.250
explore space as much as we can, but we have
to lower the risk to the astronauts as much

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:29.030
as possible.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:37.630
Nasa's fight against the cosmic invaders
continues, But the biggest mystery remains

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.090
what exactly is launching the deadliest
galactic cosmic rays?

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.520
Every second quadrillions of bits of space,
shrapnel, race towards Earth at close to the

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:51.540
speed of light.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.840
Galactic cosmic rays.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.740
The galactic cosmic rays are like a rifle
bullet.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.130
You do not want to get hit by one of these.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.250
They are invaders from outside the solar
system.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:10.150
We know they're made by something powerful
within our galaxy.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:13.560
So the source should be easy to detect.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.590
You'd think if one of them hits a detector on
Earth that we'd just be able to point back in

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.780
a straight line and say it came from over
there.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:25.830
And then look, is there something else over
there like a supernova explosion that could

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.480
explain the source of this?

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:33.810
The problem is that cosmic rays get bent as
they move by magnetic fields.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:40.980
The electric charge on a cosmic ray makes it
act like a little magnet, and the Milky Way

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:42.870
is full of other magnets.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:49.320
If I'm a cosmic ray just barreling through
the galaxy and I encounter a magnetic field,

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.840
I'm going to slightly change directions.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.860
Maybe here, maybe there, maybe up there.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:58.000
My trajectory is going to become scrambled.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:03.130
And after a few million years or so,
basically all the information about where it

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:07.300
started has been lost. It's going in a
completely random direction for all practical

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:08.260
purposes.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:15.910
But galactic cosmic rays also have a
sidekick, one that is far less elusive.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:17.530
Gamma rays.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.920
When a galactic cosmic ray hits a regular
atom out in space, it causes this big

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:24.730
reaction.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.750
In amidst all sorts of other particles,
including gamma rays, which are basically

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.420
extremely energetic photons of light.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.130
Critically, gamma rays don't get bent by
magnetic fields because they don't have an

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:42.900
electric charge, so they just beeline off in
a straight line along whatever direction the

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.210
cosmic ray was moving in in the first place.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:50.490
So we can look back at where gamma rays are
coming from in the sky.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:54.480
And that tells us where there are a lot of
cosmic rays having collisions.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.260
And they've led us to a prime suspect.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:01.930
Supernovas.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:09.140
Supernova are some of the most powerful
explosions in the universe.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:14.450
And so they're ripe grounds for these highly
energetic, extremely fast particles to be

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:15.260
created.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:21.050
When a giant star runs out of fuel, it can no
longer support its own weight.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:23.480
It collapses inward.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:31.850
Triggering a huge explosion, powerful enough
to smash atoms into tiny pieces.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:37.010
The explosion pushes out an expanding cloud
of gas and dust.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:39.200
The supernova remnant.

